WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.877
Imagine a restaurant where every
chef is a world-class AI model.

00:00:04.917 --> 00:00:08.977
You sit down once, read one menu,
and order from any of them.

00:00:09.017 --> 00:00:13.724
That restaurant is real.
It's called Amazon Bedrock.

00:00:14.083 --> 00:00:18.643
Welcome to episode one. In the next
twenty minutes, you'll get your bearings.

00:00:18.683 --> 00:00:21.943
First, what Bedrock is,
and why teams use it.

00:00:21.983 --> 00:00:24.810
Then how you pay for
it, and how you get in.

00:00:24.850 --> 00:00:29.243
Then a tour of the console, and
finally, how to read the model menu.

00:00:29.283 --> 00:00:33.362
No coding needed today. Just follow along.

00:00:33.717 --> 00:00:37.289
Chapter one. What is Bedrock, really?

00:00:37.650 --> 00:00:40.477
Here's the restaurant idea in one picture.

00:00:40.517 --> 00:00:43.377
You come through one
door, and get one menu.

00:00:43.417 --> 00:00:47.243
In the kitchen, each chef is a
model from a different company.

00:00:47.283 --> 00:00:51.540
And you never hire a chef
yourself. You just order.

00:00:51.917 --> 00:00:56.443
Meet Priya. She wants to build a
cooking app that suggests dinner ideas.

00:00:56.483 --> 00:00:58.700
She doesn't want to run AI servers,

00:00:58.740 --> 00:01:02.443
and she wants to try several
models before she picks one.

00:01:02.483 --> 00:01:05.733
We'll follow her through
this whole course.

00:01:06.083 --> 00:01:07.843
Priya has three worries.

00:01:07.883 --> 00:01:11.410
She doesn't want to run
servers. Bedrock is serverless.

00:01:11.450 --> 00:01:15.477
She wants to try many models.
Bedrock gives her one menu.

00:01:15.517 --> 00:01:20.509
And her users' data must stay
private. Bedrock doesn't share it.

00:01:20.850 --> 00:01:25.077
In AWS's words, Bedrock is
a fully managed service.

00:01:25.117 --> 00:01:29.531
It gives you many foundation
models through one API.

00:01:29.883 --> 00:01:31.610
Here's the whole picture.

00:01:31.650 --> 00:01:35.143
Your app sends a request to
Bedrock's runtime endpoint.

00:01:35.183 --> 00:01:37.777
Bedrock passes it to the model you named.

00:01:37.817 --> 00:01:41.095
And the answer comes back the same way.

00:01:41.450 --> 00:01:43.543
So what is a foundation model?

00:01:43.583 --> 00:01:47.977
It's a large model, trained ahead
of time on a huge amount of data.

00:01:48.017 --> 00:01:52.737
And it can adapt to many different
tasks, from writing to search.

00:01:53.083 --> 00:01:55.777
Bedrock is built for generative AI.

00:01:55.817 --> 00:01:58.177
That's AI that creates something new.

00:01:58.217 --> 00:02:00.510
It can write and summarize text.

00:02:00.550 --> 00:02:03.443
It can answer questions
about your documents.

00:02:03.483 --> 00:02:07.055
And it can make images and short videos.

00:02:07.417 --> 00:02:11.810
Before Bedrock, using several
AI companies was a lot of work.

00:02:11.850 --> 00:02:16.010
Each one had its own API, its
own keys, and its own bill.

00:02:16.050 --> 00:02:20.177
With Bedrock, it all runs
through your one AWS account.

00:02:20.217 --> 00:02:23.629
One set of permissions, and one bill.

00:02:23.983 --> 00:02:26.077
So what do teams build with it?

00:02:26.117 --> 00:02:29.110
Support chatbots that
answer around the clock.

00:02:29.150 --> 00:02:32.610
Moderation that flags
harmful text or images.

00:02:32.650 --> 00:02:35.877
Search that finds answers
in company documents.

00:02:35.917 --> 00:02:39.207
And pictures for products and marketing.

00:02:39.550 --> 00:02:42.543
Picture a support chatbot
on a shop's website.

00:02:42.583 --> 00:02:44.977
A customer asks where their order is.

00:02:45.017 --> 00:02:49.877
The app sends the question, with the
order details, to a model on Bedrock.

00:02:49.917 --> 00:02:55.013
The model writes a friendly answer,
and the app shows it to the customer.

00:02:55.350 --> 00:02:59.077
Without Bedrock, every model
becomes its own project.

00:02:59.117 --> 00:03:03.610
You'd rent GPUs, install the model,
and keep it running yourself.

00:03:03.650 --> 00:03:09.019
With Bedrock, AWS runs all of
that for you. It's serverless.

00:03:09.383 --> 00:03:11.443
Four reasons teams choose it.

00:03:11.483 --> 00:03:15.010
One API, the same call
style for many models.

00:03:15.050 --> 00:03:18.043
Pay per use, with no servers sitting idle.

00:03:18.083 --> 00:03:23.110
Privacy: your prompts aren't shared with
model makers or used to train models.

00:03:23.150 --> 00:03:28.110
And it plugs into the AWS
services you already use.

00:03:28.483 --> 00:03:32.810
Quick check. Who runs the servers,
when you call a model on Bedrock?

00:03:32.850 --> 00:03:38.049
AWS does. It's serverless,
so you only send requests.

00:03:38.383 --> 00:03:43.410
So far: Bedrock is one door
to many models, run by AWS.

00:03:43.450 --> 00:03:48.069
Foundation models do the work,
and your prompts stay private.

00:03:48.417 --> 00:03:52.265
Chapter two. How you
pay, and how you get in.

00:03:52.617 --> 00:03:54.743
There are three main ways to pay.

00:03:54.783 --> 00:03:58.543
On demand, you pay for each
token, with no commitment.

00:03:58.583 --> 00:04:01.677
Batch sends many requests
at once, in a file.

00:04:01.717 --> 00:04:04.610
And for some models,
batch costs about half.

00:04:04.650 --> 00:04:07.943
Provisioned throughput
reserves capacity by the hour.

00:04:07.983 --> 00:04:12.419
It's for steady, heavy traffic,
or for a custom model.

00:04:12.783 --> 00:04:16.343
On demand, what you pay for
depends on the kind of model.

00:04:16.383 --> 00:04:21.077
Text models charge for the tokens you
send, and the tokens you get back.

00:04:21.117 --> 00:04:24.477
Embedding models charge for
the tokens you send in.

00:04:24.517 --> 00:04:27.972
And image models charge per image.

00:04:28.317 --> 00:04:30.343
Here's how a batch job works.

00:04:30.383 --> 00:04:34.343
You put all your requests in
one file, in an S3 bucket.

00:04:34.383 --> 00:04:38.343
Then Bedrock works through them.
It all happens in the background.

00:04:38.383 --> 00:04:42.280
And the results land back
in S3 when it's done.

00:04:42.617 --> 00:04:45.943
Provisioned throughput is
measured in model units.

00:04:45.983 --> 00:04:49.210
Each unit gives a set number
of tokens per minute.

00:04:49.250 --> 00:04:53.543
You're billed by the hour, and a
longer commitment costs less per hour.

00:04:53.583 --> 00:04:58.787
And a model you fine-tune yourself
needs provisioned throughput to run.

00:04:59.150 --> 00:05:03.410
People often ask how Bedrock
differs from SageMaker AI.

00:05:03.450 --> 00:05:06.777
Bedrock is for building apps
with ready-made models.

00:05:06.817 --> 00:05:11.977
SageMaker AI is for training and running
your own models, with full control.

00:05:12.017 --> 00:05:17.924
A common path is to start with Bedrock,
and move only if you need more control.

00:05:18.283 --> 00:05:20.010
And there are three ways in.

00:05:20.050 --> 00:05:23.077
The console playground,
with no code at all.

00:05:23.117 --> 00:05:25.910
The AWS SDKs, from your own code.

00:05:25.950 --> 00:05:31.102
And other AWS services you
already use, like Lambda.

00:05:31.450 --> 00:05:33.310
Your Region matters too.

00:05:33.350 --> 00:05:37.877
Requests to a Region stay in that
Region, so pick one near your users.

00:05:37.917 --> 00:05:41.917
And not every model is
offered in every Region.

00:05:42.283 --> 00:05:45.343
Getting access is easier
than it used to be.

00:05:45.383 --> 00:05:46.875
Since October 2025,

00:05:46.915 --> 00:05:52.343
serverless models are switched on
by default in commercial Regions.

00:05:52.383 --> 00:05:56.843
Anthropic's models ask for a short,
one-time use case form first.

00:05:56.883 --> 00:06:02.471
And your admins still decide who
can call what, with IAM policies.

00:06:02.817 --> 00:06:05.743
IAM can be as fine-grained as you like.

00:06:05.783 --> 00:06:09.543
One team might be allowed to
call Amazon's Nova models.

00:06:09.583 --> 00:06:13.912
Another team might only be
allowed Anthropic's Claude.

00:06:14.250 --> 00:06:16.143
And what happens to your data?

00:06:16.183 --> 00:06:20.243
It isn't shared with model makers,
or used to train their models.

00:06:20.283 --> 00:06:23.410
It's encrypted on the way
in and out, and at rest.

00:06:23.450 --> 00:06:26.610
Your requests stay put,
in the Region you choose.

00:06:26.650 --> 00:06:31.206
And with PrivateLink, traffic
can skip the public internet.

00:06:31.550 --> 00:06:33.677
Here's your first-day checklist.

00:06:33.717 --> 00:06:36.043
You need an AWS account.

00:06:36.083 --> 00:06:40.177
Sign in with a user or role
that has Bedrock permissions.

00:06:40.217 --> 00:06:41.710
Choose your Region.

00:06:41.750 --> 00:06:44.839
Then open Bedrock, and you're in.

00:06:45.183 --> 00:06:47.477
Every account also has quotas.

00:06:47.517 --> 00:06:51.677
They limit how many requests and
tokens you can send each minute.

00:06:51.717 --> 00:06:55.843
If your app outgrows them, you can
ask for more in Service Quotas.

00:06:55.883 --> 00:06:58.958
So check them before a big launch.

00:06:59.317 --> 00:07:05.010
Priya starts on demand. She has no traffic
yet, so she pays only for what she uses.

00:07:05.050 --> 00:07:10.467
If her app gets busy all day, she can
move to provisioned throughput later.

00:07:10.817 --> 00:07:15.977
Here's a check. Priya has steady, heavy
traffic all day. Which way to pay fits?

00:07:16.017 --> 00:07:20.772
Provisioned throughput. It
reserves capacity by the hour.

00:07:21.150 --> 00:07:25.310
So: pay on demand, in batch,
or with provisioned capacity.

00:07:25.350 --> 00:07:32.089
Get in through the console, the SDKs,
or other services, with IAM in control.

00:07:32.417 --> 00:07:36.489
Chapter three. Finding your
way around the console.

00:07:36.850 --> 00:07:40.243
The console has a few
areas, each with one job.

00:07:40.283 --> 00:07:42.377
Find a model, and try it.

00:07:42.417 --> 00:07:44.510
Build with it, and protect it.

00:07:44.550 --> 00:07:46.918
And run it at scale.

00:07:47.250 --> 00:07:52.943
Open Bedrock in the AWS console, and
first check the Region at the top right.

00:07:52.983 --> 00:07:57.210
You can also type Bedrock into the
search bar to jump straight there.

00:07:57.250 --> 00:08:01.577
Menu names change over time,
so learn each area by its job.

00:08:01.617 --> 00:08:06.077
The overview page shows the foundation
models, and a featured model.

00:08:06.117 --> 00:08:10.488
And the model catalog sits
at the top of the left menu.

00:08:10.850 --> 00:08:14.310
The test area is where you
try models, with no code.

00:08:14.350 --> 00:08:16.943
One playground is for chat and text.

00:08:16.983 --> 00:08:19.377
Another is for images and video.

00:08:19.417 --> 00:08:24.428
And chat has a compare mode, to
test two models side by side.

00:08:24.783 --> 00:08:26.943
Here's the test area in the menu.

00:08:26.983 --> 00:08:30.610
Below it, the build area holds
the pieces of bigger apps.

00:08:30.650 --> 00:08:34.243
Agents take actions, and
flows chain steps together.

00:08:34.283 --> 00:08:40.169
Knowledge bases connect your documents,
and prompt management saves your prompts.

00:08:40.517 --> 00:08:42.743
Here's what each build tool is for.

00:08:42.783 --> 00:08:46.343
Agents carry out tasks,
like calling your APIs.

00:08:46.383 --> 00:08:50.710
Flows connect prompts, models, and
other steps into one workflow.

00:08:50.750 --> 00:08:55.591
Knowledge bases let a model
answer from your own documents.

00:08:55.917 --> 00:08:59.977
Next comes safeguards: guardrails,
and watermark detection.

00:09:00.017 --> 00:09:04.577
Then inference and assessment, for
running at scale and testing quality.

00:09:04.617 --> 00:09:09.277
That's provisioned throughput, batch,
cross-Region inference, and evaluations.

00:09:09.317 --> 00:09:14.174
And at the bottom, settings, where
you switch on things like logging.

00:09:14.550 --> 00:09:17.110
Evaluations deserve a closer look.

00:09:17.150 --> 00:09:19.410
They test models on your own task.

00:09:19.450 --> 00:09:22.943
They score the answers, so you
can compare models fairly.

00:09:22.983 --> 00:09:26.605
Then you choose with
evidence, not guesswork.

00:09:26.950 --> 00:09:29.043
Let's practise finding things.

00:09:29.083 --> 00:09:32.110
Want to try a prompt? Go to the test area.

00:09:32.150 --> 00:09:36.843
Want answers from your own documents?
That's a knowledge base, under build.

00:09:36.883 --> 00:09:40.110
Need to block harmful answers? Safeguards.

00:09:40.150 --> 00:09:44.479
Ten thousand prompts to run
overnight? Batch inference.

00:09:44.817 --> 00:09:47.777
One more check. Where would
you switch on logging?

00:09:47.817 --> 00:09:51.249
In settings, at the bottom of the menu.

00:09:51.617 --> 00:09:56.810
So the console has five jobs: find,
try, build, protect, and scale.

00:09:56.850 --> 00:10:01.361
Learn them by job, and new
menu names won't confuse you.

00:10:01.717 --> 00:10:04.940
Chapter four. Reading the menu.

00:10:05.283 --> 00:10:09.410
Open the model catalog, and you'll
find chefs from many companies.

00:10:09.450 --> 00:10:14.610
Amazon's own Nova models, Anthropic's
Claude, Meta's Llama, and Mistral.

00:10:14.650 --> 00:10:20.769
DeepSeek, Stability AI, Cohere, and
more, over a hundred models in all.

00:10:21.117 --> 00:10:23.443
Each family has its own focus.

00:10:23.483 --> 00:10:27.543
Amazon's Nova models cover
text, images, and video.

00:10:27.583 --> 00:10:32.343
Anthropic's Claude reads text and
images, with a very long context.

00:10:32.383 --> 00:10:34.977
Meta's Llama models are open-weight.

00:10:35.017 --> 00:10:38.978
And Stability AI focuses on images.

00:10:39.350 --> 00:10:43.243
Like a real menu, the catalog
lets you narrow things down.

00:10:43.283 --> 00:10:48.709
Filter by provider, by what the model
makes, or by the task you need.

00:10:49.083 --> 00:10:51.877
Let's walk through the
catalog page with Priya.

00:10:51.917 --> 00:10:55.143
At the top, a search box
finds a model by name.

00:10:55.183 --> 00:10:57.777
On the left, she filters by provider.

00:10:57.817 --> 00:11:01.010
Then by modality, what
goes in and comes out.

00:11:01.050 --> 00:11:04.677
And by task, like chat,
summarizing, or search.

00:11:04.717 --> 00:11:11.093
Each card shows the provider, the model,
and its status, like active or legacy.

00:11:11.450 --> 00:11:14.143
Models also differ by what they make.

00:11:14.183 --> 00:11:18.310
Text models, the large language
models, read and write words.

00:11:18.350 --> 00:11:22.577
Image models paint pictures, and
video models film short clips.

00:11:22.617 --> 00:11:26.377
Multimodal models can look at
a picture and describe it.

00:11:26.417 --> 00:11:30.895
And embedding models turn
text into numbers for search.

00:11:31.250 --> 00:11:35.477
In the catalog, modality means
what goes in, and what comes out.

00:11:35.517 --> 00:11:37.777
Text in and text out, for chat.

00:11:37.817 --> 00:11:40.477
Text in and an image out, for pictures.

00:11:40.517 --> 00:11:44.895
And an image in with text
out, to describe a photo.

00:11:45.250 --> 00:11:47.710
Embeddings turn meaning into numbers.

00:11:47.750 --> 00:11:52.271
So search can find ideas,
not just matching words.

00:11:52.617 --> 00:11:55.410
Here's an embedding model
at work in a search.

00:11:55.450 --> 00:11:58.577
Your question is turned
into a list of numbers.

00:11:58.617 --> 00:12:02.577
Those numbers are compared with
the numbers of every document.

00:12:02.617 --> 00:12:07.828
And the closest documents come back,
even when they use different words.

00:12:08.183 --> 00:12:12.043
Every model has an ID, like
a dish number on the menu.

00:12:12.083 --> 00:12:15.208
Some models are reached
through inference profiles,

00:12:15.248 --> 00:12:17.510
which route requests across Regions.

00:12:17.550 --> 00:12:23.410
A geographic profile keeps your data
inside one area, like the US or the EU.

00:12:23.450 --> 00:12:28.627
And not every model is in every
Region, so check before you build.

00:12:28.983 --> 00:12:33.210
You'll see those profiles as a
short prefix on the model ID.

00:12:33.250 --> 00:12:36.477
With no prefix, the request
stays in your Region.

00:12:36.517 --> 00:12:39.577
U S dot, routes within the United States.

00:12:39.617 --> 00:12:44.005
And global dot, can route
anywhere there's capacity.

00:12:44.350 --> 00:12:47.377
Click any model, and its model card opens.

00:12:47.417 --> 00:12:52.277
Take Amazon Nova 2 Lite. It
reads text, images, and video.

00:12:52.317 --> 00:12:56.910
It writes text, and its context
window holds about a million tokens.

00:12:56.950 --> 00:13:01.276
And the card gives you the
model ID to use in code.

00:13:01.650 --> 00:13:03.510
What's a context window?

00:13:03.550 --> 00:13:08.796
It's how much text the model can work
with at once, counted in tokens.

00:13:09.117 --> 00:13:11.277
How big is a million tokens?

00:13:11.317 --> 00:13:15.243
For Claude, it's roughly seven
hundred and fifty thousand words.

00:13:15.283 --> 00:13:20.141
That's more than two and a half
thousand pages, in a single prompt.

00:13:20.483 --> 00:13:22.877
Models also have a life cycle.

00:13:22.917 --> 00:13:26.443
An active model gets updates,
and you can build on it.

00:13:26.483 --> 00:13:31.377
When it turns legacy, you usually get
six months' notice before it goes away.

00:13:31.417 --> 00:13:36.243
After end of life, calls to it fail,
so plan your move to a newer version.

00:13:36.283 --> 00:13:41.233
The model card tells you the
earliest date a model can retire.

00:13:41.583 --> 00:13:44.277
Priya opens the Nova 2 Lite card.

00:13:44.317 --> 00:13:48.143
She checks what it reads and
writes, and its context window.

00:13:48.183 --> 00:13:52.243
She copies the model ID, because
her code will need it later.

00:13:52.283 --> 00:13:57.166
And one click opens the model in
the playground, ready to try.

00:13:57.517 --> 00:14:00.010
Some models are reasoning models.

00:14:00.050 --> 00:14:03.753
Before answering, they think
through the problem step by step,

00:14:03.793 --> 00:14:05.443
and can show that thinking.

00:14:05.483 --> 00:14:12.009
DeepSeek R1 is one example. It's
slower, but strong on tricky problems.

00:14:12.350 --> 00:14:17.043
Beyond the main menu, there's a specialty
aisle called Bedrock Marketplace.

00:14:17.083 --> 00:14:20.277
It adds over a hundred
more specialized models.

00:14:20.317 --> 00:14:25.947
Those run on endpoints you deploy, while
serverless models are ready to call.

00:14:26.283 --> 00:14:27.877
So how do you choose?

00:14:27.917 --> 00:14:31.510
Start from the job: text,
pictures, or search.

00:14:31.550 --> 00:14:34.577
Smaller models are often
faster and cheaper.

00:14:34.617 --> 00:14:38.689
Then try a few in the
playground before you commit.

00:14:39.050 --> 00:14:41.377
Priya makes a shortlist for her app.

00:14:41.417 --> 00:14:44.710
For dinner ideas, a
small, fast text model.

00:14:44.750 --> 00:14:47.610
For dish photos later on, an image model.

00:14:47.650 --> 00:14:51.715
And to search her recipes,
an embedding model.

00:14:52.050 --> 00:14:55.543
Before we wrap up, four
first-day mistakes to avoid.

00:14:55.583 --> 00:14:59.110
The model isn't showing? You
may be in the wrong Region.

00:14:59.150 --> 00:15:03.143
A Claude call fails? Fill in
the one-time Anthropic form.

00:15:03.183 --> 00:15:06.810
Access denied? Your role
needs Bedrock permissions.

00:15:06.850 --> 00:15:12.034
And a model retiring? Check its
model card, and plan your move.

00:15:12.383 --> 00:15:16.543
Here's a question. Which kind of
model would power document search?

00:15:16.583 --> 00:15:21.505
An embedding model. It compares
meaning, not exact words.

00:15:21.850 --> 00:15:25.477
Now it's your turn. Before
the next episode, try this.

00:15:25.517 --> 00:15:28.677
Open the Bedrock console,
and check your Region.

00:15:28.717 --> 00:15:32.377
Find the model catalog,
and filter by text models.

00:15:32.417 --> 00:15:35.910
Open one model card,
and note its model ID.

00:15:35.950 --> 00:15:39.954
And find where the playground
and the guardrails live.

00:15:40.317 --> 00:15:42.377
Here are the new words from today.

00:15:42.417 --> 00:15:45.843
A token is a small piece
of text that you pay for.

00:15:45.883 --> 00:15:49.443
A model ID names the
exact model and version.

00:15:49.483 --> 00:15:53.177
An inference profile routes
your request across Regions.

00:15:53.217 --> 00:15:57.566
And provisioned throughput
reserves capacity by the hour.

00:15:57.917 --> 00:16:03.743
That's episode one. Bedrock is one door to
many models, paid per use, and private.

